# Ubuntu 22.04's tr is GNU coreutils 8.32: translate, delete, squeeze, complement, the repeat and
# class constructs, and every refusal wording. Each reply was produced by `bash -c` in an
# ubuntu:22.04 container (coreutils 8.32-4.1ubuntu1.4, LANG=C.UTF-8 as the persona's login sets
# it), standard input closed. Where the reply differs from the toybox tr of the Android persona the
# line is one that tells them apart: `-C` complements here, `\x41` and `\e` are not escapes here,
# `[y*]` repeats here, and the operand and option errors name `Try 'tr --help'`.
# source: ubuntu:22.04 container run of /usr/bin/tr, 8.32-4.1ubuntu1.4 (synthetic command shapes)
# date: 2026-10-08
# protocol: ssh
# persona: ubuntu
$ printf 'Hello World\n' | tr a-z A-Z; echo "rc=$?"
> HELLO WORLD
> rc=0
$ printf 'Hello World\n' | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]'; echo "rc=$?"
> HELLO WORLD
> rc=0
$ printf 'Hello World\n' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'; echo "rc=$?"
